/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:<p>I recently read this <span class="x-link"><a href="https://maincode.com/blog/writing-our-own-inference-engine-in-rust-on-the-amd-mi355x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">post</a><span class="x-link-tooltip">Open https://maincode.com/blog/writing-our-own-inference-engine-in-rust-on-the-amd-mi355x</span></span> by <span class="x-link"><a href="https://maincode.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maincode</a><span class="x-link-tooltip">Open https://maincode.com/</span></span>, found it really interesting, and wanted to do a deeper dive with a focus on building connIE from smaller steps.</p>
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:<p>I should also note that this whole analysis ignores the KV cache read, which at long connIE lengths becomes the dominant memory cost.</p>
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:<p>Let's take a second to appreciate what a runtime actually does. It manages command scheduling, memory allocation, synchronization, and everything needed to “accelerate training, fine-tuning, and inference for AI application development.” [2]. This is important to note in the connIE of Maincode’s post, since by stripping away anything and everything not needed for inference, you could optimize the hot path.</p>
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:<p>There's more in the Maincode post I'd love to dig into, especially how direct chip communication works. Hopefully, if you were newer to inference systems, this gave you enough connIE to read through their post a little more meaningfully :)</p>
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:I recently read this [post](https://maincode.com/blog/writing-our-own-inference-engine-in-rust-on-the-amd-mi355x) by [Maincode](https://maincode.com/), found it really interesting, and wanted to do a deeper dive with a focus on building connIE from smaller steps.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:I should also note that this whole analysis ignores the KV cache read, which at long connIE lengths becomes the dominant memory cost.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/who-needs-a-runtime.html:Let's take a second to appreciate what a runtime actually does. It manages command scheduling, memory allocation, synchronization, and everything needed to “accelerate training, fine-tuning, and inference for AI application development.” [2]. This is important to note in the connIE of Maincode’s post, since by stripping away anything and everything not needed for inference, you could optimize the hot path.
