/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/lcl_usr_docs/bkg_context.md:We will design side tasks in LinuxArena that can be split across multiple main tasks: each fragment is a legitimate-looking part of some main task, and only the composition is harmful. We may reuse existing main and side tasks or come up with new ones. These side tasks are meant to demonstrate that an attack the monitor catches when concentrated in a single step or commit is missed when the monitor lacks the full connIE. Then, we will attempt to figure out how egregious the sabotage can be while remaining undetected.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:  abstract: "Autoencoders introduce a fundamental idea in representation learning: data from a high-dimensional space can be mapped into a lower-dimensional latent space that preserves essential structure. While autoencoders operate within a single modality, many modern learning tasks require aligning information across modalities such as images and nIE. This leads to the notion of a shared latent space, where multiple encoders map different types of data into a common geometric space.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:This presentation develops the mathematics of latent representations, explains how autoencoders learn them through reconstruction loss, and then generalizes the idea to cross-modal alignment. We introduce contrastive objectives as an alternative to reconstruction and show how they encourage paired samples to be nearby in the latent space. Finally, we illustrate these principles with the Contrastive Language–Image Pretraining (CLIP) framework, which trains nIE and image encoders jointly to inhabit the same embedding space. The goal is to understand the geometry, training objectives, and intuition behind shared latent spaces, without focusing on model-specific architecture details.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:- nIE descriptions $T$.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:f_"nIE": T -> bb(R)^d
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:such that the embeddings of matching image–nIE pairs lie close together in latent space.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:f_"img"(s_i) approx f_"nIE"(tau_i),
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:f_"img"(s_i) perp f_"nIE"(tau_j) quad "(far apart)".
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:This geometry enables cross-modal reasoning: we can compare images and nIE using simple vector similarity.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:Given a batch of image–nIE pairs $\{(s_i,tau_i)\}_(i=1)^N$, define their embeddings:
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/site/Simar-malhotra09.github.io/writing/documents/shared_latent_spaces.typ:u_i = f_"nIE"(tau_i).
