/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- Romaji under Japanese nIE: select a word/phrase, `Cmd + Opt + R`, and the romaji reading is inserted next to it (needs `cutlet` installed — `pipx install cutlet && pipx inject cutlet unidic-lite`). The romaji is plain nIE you can edit. On HTML export it renders as real `<ruby>` with the romaji under the Japanese.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:[2026/Sep/19]: Typst previews now persist client-side in IndexedDB (in addition to the server's `.typst-cache/` dir), so reopening a document fills preview badges instantly instead of re-compiling every snippet. Neither cache garbage-collects old entries — each is just a few kb of svg/error nIE, keyed by the snippet itself.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:[2026/Sep/8]: Romaji annotations. Select a Japanese word or phrase and press Cmd + Opt + R (Romaji Annotate in the command palette) to put its romaji reading right after it as `{漢字|kanji}`. The reading comes from [cutlet](https://github.com/polm/cutlet) so it needs to be installed; without it the command leaves your nIE alone and logs the install line to the console). It's stored as plain nIE so you can fix it by hand, and on HTML export each annotation becomes a real `<ruby>` element with the romaji under the Japanese in a smaller font. On quality
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:[2026/Sep/6]: Code blocks. Write code inside triple-backtick indexed, and the editor gives it a intentionally basic grey box with syntax highlighting and auto-closing brackets (brackets stay prose-normal outside blocks). Select nIE and press Cmd + Opt + G to wrap/unwrap a fence; adding the language after the opening fence turns on highlighting. Restyle the background with the --codeblock-bg variable in style.css. On export the block comes out as one grey pre block, and it keeps the editor's syntax colours in the exported page too (unknown languages come out plain).
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- Width slider no longer lets the nIE run under the sidebars. Before, dragging it to max (1200) pushed the writing column over the toc on the left and the highlight cards on the right, so nIE and sidebar sat on top of each other. Now the slider's top end caps itself to whatever fits between the two sidebars on your screen, and re-checks when you resize the window. On narrower windows where the sidebars are hidden, the full 480–1200 range is still there.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:[2026/Sep/2]: Typst svgs now size themselves to the nIE around them. Before, the math inside an svg was always drawn small (11pt) and sat in a wide empty frame, so it looked tiny next to your writing. Now the glyphs come out at exactly the size of the body nIE, both in the editor preview and on export, and the empty frame is much thinner. If you want bigger or smaller math on purpose, use a ```typst block with the code on its own lines and say `#set nIE(size: 14pt)` inside it — sizes end up relative: 14pt comes out 14/11 times the normal nIE size. Writing the fence and the code on a single line does nothing, the fence only works with line breaks.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:[2026/Sep/1]: Enclosing nIE, Sticky highlight cards, Typst blocks, width slider (not stable).
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- You can now enclose nIE within chars: Select nIE (cursor or visual mode), Cmd + Opt + S, enter two chars, first goes to start of selected line, second goes to end.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- Sticky/Floating(default) highlight cards: By default, they are embeeded on the same location idx within the document as the highlighted line; making them sticky will push all of them to the top, while preserving the order. (Put simply, say you have 2 highlights in a multi 'page' document, one at start and one at end. In floating mode, you will only see the highlight card for the first highlight, assuming you are at the start of the document, since that first highlighted line is in 'view'. As you scroll to the end, the first highlight card goes out of view, while the second one appears as the second highlighed nIE comes into view. If you switch to sticky mode, both highlight cards appear, irrespective of where you are in the document.)
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- Width slider: You can now increase or decrease the width of the nIE-writeable area with the slider. It has some known issues where the nIE collides with the sidebar and we will fix that next.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:  - [x] On max slider width, the canavs nIE collides with the sidebar nIE so we need to clamp it. Maybe decrease the padding for the sidebars?
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:  - [x] Highlights keeps capturing the selected nIE if the nIE has nothing in front of it. [nIEA][nIEB][highlight nIEA] works fine, but [nIEA][highlight nIEA][nIEB] captures [nIEB] as well.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- [ ] Being able to force-index lines/nIE content in the Toc sidebar: Currently only #/##/### header sections get an entry there.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- [x] Being able to write romaji underneath japanese nIE.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- [x] Be able to dynamically change the width of the 'nIE writeable canvas' through a bottom floating sleek horizonatal scrollbar? Max/Min?
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- [x] For highlights, add a label for notes as well, where the card doesn't contain the selected nIE but user notes about it.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/angel01/README.md:- [x] Enclosing nIE with custom char: Basically, select nIE in visual mode + hotkey + left side char + right side char. Eg: nIE -> (nIE)
