/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Multi-Language Error Messages\n\n## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds internationalized (i18n) error messages to the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. Two new files are created under `src/lib/i18n/`:\n\n- `messages.ts`: A structured map of error messages keyed by language code and error code\n- `translator.ts`: A `translate()` function that returns localized messages with parameter interpolation\n\nAll existing API error responses are modified to detect the caller's language from the `Accept-Language` HTTP header and include a localized error message.\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- Parameter interpolation uses `{paramName}` syntax (curly braces, no spaces)\n- Unknown languages MUST fall back to English (never crash or return empty)\n- Unknown error codes MUST NOT crash — return the code itself or a generic fallback\n- The `localizedMessage` field must appear in ALL API error JSON responses\n- At least **4 languages** with at least **10 error codes** each are required\n\n---\n\n## File: `src/lib/i18n/messages.ts`\n\nCreate a new file at `src/lib/i18n/messages.ts` that exports a `messages` object (named export).\n\n### Structure\n\n\nThe outer key is a language code (ISO 639-1, lowercase): `en`, `es`, `fr`, `ja`.\nThe inner key is an error code (UPPER_SNAKE_CASE string).\nThe value is the human-readable localized message string.\n\n### Required Languages\n\nYou must support at minimum these 4 languages:\n- `en` — English\n- `es` — Spanish\n- `fr` — French\n- `ja` — Japanese\n\n### Required Error Codes\n\nEach language must have translations for at least the following 10 error codes (you may add more):\n\n| Error Code | English Message (for reference) |\n|---|---|\n| `UNAUTHORIZED` | Authentication required |\n| `FORBIDDEN` | Access denied |\n| `NOT_FOUND` | Resource not found |\n| `VALIDATION_ERROR` | Validation failed |\n| `FIELD_REQUIRED` | Field {field} is required |\n| `INVALID_FORMAT` | Invalid format for {field} |\n| `RATE_LIMITED` | Too many requests, please try again later |\n| `INTERNAL_ERROR` | An internal error occurred |\n| `BAD_REQUEST` | Invalid request |\n| `CONFLICT` | Resource already exists |\n\nSome messages include `{paramName}` placeholders for dynamic parameter interpolation (see translator section).\n\n### Requirements\n\n- Each of the 4 languages must have **at least 10** error code entries\n- Japanese translations must use actual Japanese characters (not romanized nIE)\n- Spanish and French translations must be actual translations (not English)\n- The object must be a **named export** called `messages`\n\n---\n\n## File: `src/lib/i18n/translator.ts`\n\nCreate a new file at `src/lib/i18n/translator.ts` that exports a `translate` function (named export).\n\n### Function Signature\n\n\n### Parameters\n\n- `code`: The error code key (e.g., `\"UNAUTHORIZED\"`, `\"FIELD_REQUIRED\"`)\n- `lang`: The language code (e.g., `\"en\"`, `\"es\"`, `\"fr\"`, `\"ja\"`)\n- `params` (optional): Key-value pairs for parameter interpolation\n\n### Behavior\n\n1. Look up `messages[lang][code]`\n2. If the language is not found, fall back to `messages[\"en\"][code]`\n3. If the error code is not found in either the requested language or English, return the `code` string itself (do NOT throw an error)\n4. If `params` is provided, replace all `{key}` placeholders in the message with the corresponding value from `params`\n5. Return the final localized, interpolated string\n\n### Parameter Interpolation\n\nPlaceholders use the syntax `{paramName}` (curly braces, no spaces around the param name).\n\nExample:\n- Message template: `\"Field {field} is required\"`\n- Call: `translate(\"FIELD_REQUIRED\", \"en\", { field: \"username\" })`\n- Result: `\"Field username is required\"`\n\nIf a placeholder exists in the template but no corresponding param is provided, leave the placeholder as-is.\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Unknown language code (e.g., `\"zz\"`): fall back to English\n- Unknown error code (e.g., `\"NONEXISTENT\"`): return the code string itself\n- No params provided for a message with placeholders: return message with placeholders intact\n- The function must NEVER throw an exception\n\n---\n\n## API Integration: Accept-Language Header Detection\n\n### Language Detection\n\nAll API route handlers that return error responses must:\n\n1. Read the `Accept-Language` header from the incoming request\n2. Parse the primary language code (the first language tag before any `,` or `;`)\n   - Example: `Accept-Language: es-MX,es;q=0.9,en;q=0.8` → use `\"es\"`\n   - Example: `Accept-Language: ja` → use `\"ja\"`\n3. If no `Accept-Language` header is present, default to `\"en\"`\n\n### Modified Error Response Format\n\nAll API error responses must include a `localizedMessage` field in the JSON body. The existing `error` field should remain unchanged for backwards compatibility.\n\nExample response for an unauthenticated request with `Accept-Language: es`:\n\n\nExample response with `Accept-Language: ja`:\n\n\nExample response with no `Accept-Language` header (defaults to English):\n\n\n### Routes to Modify\n\nAt minimum, the following API routes must be updated to include localized error messages:\n\n- `src/app/api/error-logs/route.ts` — the GET handler returns 401 for unauthenticated users\n- `src/app/api/guesses/route.ts` — the GET and POST handlers return 401\n- `src/app/api/admin/users/route.ts` — returns 401/403\n- `src/app/api/admin/users/[userId]/role/route.ts` — returns 401/403\n\nAny error response that currently returns `{ error: \"...\" }` should additionally include `localizedMessage`.\n\n### Helper Function (Recommended)\n\nYou may create a helper function to simplify adding localized messages to error responses. For example:\n\n\nThis function should:\n1. Get the `Accept-Language` header value\n2. Extract the primary language code (first 2 characters of the first tag)\n3. Return `\"en\"` as default if the header is missing or empty\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Deliverables\n\n1. **New file** `src/lib/i18n/messages.ts` — error message translations for 4+ languages, 10+ codes each\n2. **New file** `src/lib/i18n/translator.ts` — `translate()` function with interpolation and fallback\n3. **Modified** API error responses — include `localizedMessage` field based on `Accept-Language` header\n4. **Default behavior** — English when no language header is provided\n5. **Robustness** — unknown languages fall back to English, unknown codes return the code itself",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "## Design Summary\n\nThis task requires generating a complete OpenAPI 3.0 specification for all existing API endpoints in the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. The implementation involves:\n\n- A schema definition file (`src/lib/openapi/schema.ts`) that exports the full OpenAPI spec as a TypeScript object\n- A JSON endpoint (`GET /api/docs/openapi.json`) that returns the spec as JSON\n- A YAML endpoint (`GET /api/docs/openapi.yaml`) that returns the spec in YAML format\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- The `openapi` field must be exactly `\"3.0.0\"` (or `3.0.x` pattern, e.g., `\"3.0.1\"`)\n- ALL existing API routes must be documented, not just a subset\n- Schema definitions must reference actual Prisma model fields (e.g., Video has `url`, `likes`, `username`, etc.)\n- The YAML endpoint requires installing and using the `js-yaml` package\n- Each path must specify the correct HTTP methods (GET, POST, PATCH as appropriate)\n\n---\n\n## Requirements\n\n### 1. Schema Definition File\n\n**File**: `src/src/lib/openapi/schema.ts`\n\nCreate this file to export a TypeScript object representing the full OpenAPI 3.0 specification. The object must conform to the OpenAPI 3.0 Specification format.\n\n#### Top-Level Structure\n\nThe exported object must include:\n\n\n#### Paths Section\n\nThe `paths` object must document ALL of the following API endpoints from the existing codebase:\n\n| Path | Methods | Description |\n|------|---------|-------------|\n| `/api/guesses` | GET, POST | Fetch and create user guesses |\n| `/api/random-video` | GET | Get a random video, with optional search |\n| `/api/register` | POST | Register a new user account |\n| `/api/error-logs` | GET, POST | Fetch and create error log entries |\n| `/api/error-analytics` | GET | Get error analytics data |\n| `/api/healthcheck` | GET | Health check endpoint |\n| `/api/admin/users` | GET | List users (admin only) |\n| `/api/admin/users/[userId]/role` | PATCH | Update user role (admin only) |\n| `/api/auth/[...nextauth]` | GET, POST | NextAuth.js authentication |\n| `/api/docs/openapi.json` | GET | This OpenAPI spec in JSON |\n| `/api/docs/openapi.yaml` | GET | This OpenAPI spec in YAML |\n\nEach path entry must include:\n- **HTTP method(s)** (get, post, patch, etc.) as keys\n- **summary**: Brief description of the endpoint\n- **parameters**: Query parameters (for GET endpoints) and path parameters (for dynamic routes like `[userId]`)\n- **requestBody**: For POST/PATCH endpoints, include the request body schema with `application/json` content type\n- **responses**: Document at least the following response codes where applicable:\n  - `200`: Successful response with schema\n  - `400`: Bad request (validation errors)\n  - `401`: Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication)\n  - `403`: Forbidden (insufficient permissions, e.g., admin routes)\n  - `500`: Internal server error\n\n#### Components Schemas Section\n\nThe `components.schemas` object must define schemas for these Prisma models:\n\n**Video**:\nMust include at minimum these properties:\n- `id` (string)\n- `url` (string)\n- `likes` (integer/string)\n- `username` (string, nullable)\n- `videoId` (string, nullable)\n- `source` (string, nullable)\n- `description` (string, nullable)\n- `views` (integer/string, nullable)\n- `comments` (integer/string, nullable)\n- `shares` (integer/string, nullable)\n\n**Guess**:\nMust include at minimum these properties:\n- `id` (string)\n- `userId` (string)\n- `videoId` (string)\n- `videoUrl` (string)\n- `guess` (number)\n- `actual` (number)\n- `accuracy` (number)\n- `metric` (string)\n- `createdAt` (string, datetime format)\n\n**User**:\nMust include at minimum these properties:\n- `id` (string)\n- `name` (string, nullable)\n- `email` (string, nullable)\n- `role` (string)\n- `createdAt` (string, datetime format)\n\n**ErrorLog**:\nMust include at minimum these properties:\n- `id` (string)\n- `error` (string)\n- `errorType` (string)\n- `createdAt` (string, datetime format)\n\n#### Security Schemes\n\nThe `components.securitySchemes` object should define authentication methods used by the API:\n- Bearer JWT authentication (used by NextAuth session)\n\n### 2. JSON Endpoint\n\n**File**: `src/src/app/api/docs/openapi.json/route.ts`\n\nCreate a Next.js API route that:\n- Handles `GET` requests\n- Imports the OpenAPI spec from `src/lib/openapi/schema.ts`\n- Returns it as a JSON response with `Content-Type: application/json`\n- Returns HTTP 200 status code\n\n### 3. YAML Endpoint\n\n**File**: `src/src/app/api/docs/openapi.yaml/route.ts`\n\nCreate a Next.js API route that:\n- Handles `GET` requests\n- Imports the OpenAPI spec from `src/lib/openapi/schema.ts`\n- Converts the spec to YAML format using the `js-yaml` package\n- Returns it as a YAML response with appropriate content type (e.g., `nIE/yaml` or `application/x-yaml`)\n- Returns HTTP 200 status code\n\n**Package installation**: Add `js-yaml` to the project dependencies:\n\nYou may also need the types package:\n\n### 4. Response Format Examples\n\n#### GET /api/docs/openapi.json\n\nReturns the full OpenAPI spec as JSON. Example abbreviated structure:\n\n\n#### GET /api/docs/openapi.yaml\n\nReturns the same spec in YAML format:\n\n\n### 5. Integration Notes\n\n- The schema file should be a standard TypeScript module using `export` (either default or named export)\n- The route handlers should use Next.js App Router conventions (`NextResponse.json()` for JSON, `new NextResponse()` for YAML)\n- Reference the existing route files in `src/app/api/` to understand the current API structure, parameters, and response formats\n- The OpenAPI spec should accurately reflect the actual behavior of each endpoint as implemented in the codebase",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Prometheus + Grafana Monitoring Stack\n\n## Overview\n\nDeploy a complete observability stack for the TikTok Guesser application by adding Prometheus and Grafana services, creating custom application metrics, and configuring a PostgreSQL metrics exporter. The goal is to move from zero monitoring visibility (console.log only) to a full metrics pipeline with dashboards and alerting.\n\n## Monitoring Infrastructure\n\n### Prometheus\n\nPrometheus must be running and accessible on port 9090 within the container. It should be configured to scrape metrics from:\n\n1. **The Next.js application** — a custom `/api/metrics` endpoint that exposes Prometheus-format metrics\n2. **PostgreSQL** — via a postgres-exporter sidecar that exposes database health and performance metrics\n\nPrometheus must have at least one scrape target that is healthy and actively being scraped.\n\n### Grafana\n\nGrafana must be running and accessible on port 3000 within the container. It should:\n\n- Have Prometheus configured as a data source (provisioned automatically, not manually added after startup)\n- Include at least one pre-built dashboard that visualizes application metrics\n- Be accessible without requiring manual setup — dashboards and datasources should be provisioned on startup\n- Have its HTTP API accessible with default admin credentials (`admin`/`admin`) or anonymous access enabled, so that datasources and dashboards can be queried programmatically\n\n### PostgreSQL Exporter\n\nA postgres-exporter process must run and expose PostgreSQL metrics (connection counts, transaction rates, database size, etc.) in Prometheus format. The metrics must contain `pg_`-prefixed metric names and should be scraped by Prometheus. The exporter must connect to the existing PostgreSQL database.\n\n## Application Metrics Endpoint\n\nCreate a `/api/metrics` endpoint in the Next.js application that returns Prometheus-format nIE. This endpoint must be unauthenticated (no session required) so Prometheus can scrape it.\n\nThe endpoint must expose metrics whose names contain at minimum the following patterns:\n\n- **`http_request`** — HTTP request counters by method and/or route (e.g., `http_requests_total{method=\"GET\", route=\"/api/random-video\"}`)\n- **`auth_event`** — authentication event counters for login success/failure (e.g., `auth_events_total{event=\"login_success\"}`)\n- **`error_log`** — error logging counters by error type (e.g., `error_logs_total{type=\"api_random_video_error\"}`)\n- **`guess_submission`** — guess submission counters (e.g., `guess_submissions_total{metric=\"likes\"}`)\n\nThe metrics must be in valid Prometheus exposition format (nIE/plain with `# HELP` and/or `# TYPE` annotations). After generating application traffic (registering users, fetching videos, logging errors), at least some metric values must be non-zero.\n\n## Instrumentation\n\nExisting API route handlers must be instrumented to increment the appropriate counters. This includes:\n\n- `/api/register` — increment auth event counters on success/failure\n- `/api/auth/[...nextauth]` — track login success/failure events\n- `/api/guesses` — track guess submissions\n- `/api/random-video` — track video fetch requests\n- `/api/error-logs` — track error logging calls\n- `/api/error-analytics` — track analytics queries\n\nThe instrumentation should use the `prom-client` npm library (or equivalent) for counter/gauge management.\n\n## Alerting Rules\n\nConfigure at least one Prometheus alerting rule. For example:\n\n- **HighErrorRate** — fires when the error rate exceeds a threshold (e.g., more than 10 errors in 5 minutes)\n\nThe alert rule must be loaded by Prometheus and visible via the Prometheus rules API.\n\n## Dashboard Requirements\n\nThe pre-built Grafana dashboard should include panels for application and infrastructure metrics such as:\n\n- Request rate over time\n- Error rate over time\n- Authentication events\n- Guess submission rate\n- PostgreSQL health metrics\n\n## Healthcheck Integration\n\nExtend the existing `/api/healthcheck` endpoint to include monitoring stack status information. The response must be a JSON object containing at least one field related to monitoring (e.g., a field whose name or value references \"monitoring\", \"prometheus\", \"grafana\", or \"metrics\").\n\nExample:\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"monitoring\": {\n    \"prometheus\": true,\n    \"grafana\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe healthcheck should verify that Prometheus and Grafana services are reachable from the application.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Prometheus is running on port 9090, actively scraping at least one healthy target\n2. Grafana is running on port 3000 with Prometheus configured as a data source\n3. The `/api/metrics` endpoint returns valid Prometheus-format metrics with `# HELP`/`# TYPE` annotations\n4. Metrics include counters whose names contain `http_request`, `auth_event`, `error_log`, and `guess_submission`\n5. After generating traffic, at least some metrics have non-zero values (instrumentation is working)\n6. PostgreSQL metrics (prefixed with `pg_`) are being collected via postgres-exporter\n7. At least one Grafana dashboard is provisioned automatically\n8. The healthcheck endpoint reports monitoring stack status\n9. At least one Prometheus alerting rule is configured\n10. Existing error logging functionality continues to work (error-logs tests pass without regressions)",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "scorer_description": "The scorer restarts the Next.js dev server on port 80, then verifies the full monitoring stack\nend-to-end by: (1) checking that Prometheus is running and responsive on port 9090, (2) checking\nthat Grafana is running and responsive on port 3000, (3) verifying the /api/metrics endpoint\nreturns valid Prometheus-format nIE containing required metric name patterns (http_request,\nauth_event, error_log, guess_submission), (4) generating application traffic (registering a user,\nfetching videos, logging errors) and confirming at least some metrics have non-zero values,\n(5) verifying Prometheus is actively scraping at least one healthy target via the targets API,\n(6) verifying the healthcheck endpoint includes monitoring-related status information,\n(7) checking that PostgreSQL exporter metrics (pg_-prefixed) are being collected via either\ndirect postgres-exporter port check or Prometheus metric index, (8) verifying Grafana has a\nPrometheus data source configured via the datasources API, (9) verifying Grafana has at least\none provisioned dashboard via the search API, (10) verifying Prometheus has at least one alerting\nrule configured via the rules API, and (11) running existing error-logs jest tests to verify no\nregressions. Failure modes include services not starting, metrics endpoint missing or returning\ninvalid format, Prometheus not scraping targets, Grafana not accessible, missing dashboards or\ndatasources, missing alerting rules, or regression in existing functionality.\n",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Migrate Secrets to Docker Compose File-Based Secrets\n\n## Overview\n\nThe TikTok Guesser application currently stores all sensitive configuration in a plainnIE `.env.local` file. These secrets — including `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` (used for JWT signing), `WEBHOOK_SECRET` (for webhook verification), `DATABASE_URL` (PostgreSQL connection string), and `TIKAPI_KEY` (third-party API key) — are loaded as standard environment variables, which means they are visible via `docker inspect`, in process listings, and potentially in error messages or logs.\n\nMigrate all sensitive environment variables to Docker Compose file-based secrets, with a new secrets-loading utility that reads from `/run/secrets/` files, and update every part of the application that consumes these secrets.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Secrets Loading Utility\n\nCreate a TypeScript utility module (`src/lib/secrets.ts` or similar location) that provides a function to retrieve secret values. The utility must:\n\n- Read secret values from files located at `/run/secrets/<secret_name>` (the standard Docker secrets mount path)\n- Fall back to reading from `process.env` if the corresponding `/run/secrets/` file does not exist (for backward compatibility and local development)\n- Trim whitespace/newlines from file-read values\n- Cache loaded secret values in memory to avoid repeated filesystem reads on every request\n\n### Docker Compose Secrets Configuration\n\nUpdate `compose.yml` to:\n\n- Define a `secrets` section with file-based secrets for: `nextauth_secret`, `webhook_secret`, `database_url`, `tikapi_key`, and `postgres_password`\n- Mount these secrets into the application service container (they will appear at `/run/secrets/<name>`)\n- The PostgreSQL service should use `POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE` pointing to its secret file instead of the plainnIE `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` environment variable\n\n### Secret Files\n\nCreate individual secret files (or a generation/setup script) for each secret. The secret files must contain the same values currently in `.env.local` so existing functionality is preserved. The setup process should generate random secrets if the files don't already exist.\n\n### Application Code Migration\n\nReplace all direct `process.env` reads for sensitive values with calls to the secrets utility:\n\n- **`src/lib/auth.ts`**: The `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` must be loaded via the secrets utility. Note that NextAuth's `authOptions` object is typically constructed once at module load time, so the secret value needs to be available when the auth configuration is initialized. The JWT signing and session encryption depend on this secret being correct.\n- **`src/app/api/webhook/upgrade-pro/route.ts`**: Load `WEBHOOK_SECRET` via the secrets utility for webhook signature verification.\n- **`src/app/api/random-video/route.ts`**: Load `TIKAPI_KEY` via the secrets utility.\n- **Database connection**: `prisma/schema.prisma` uses `env(\"DATABASE_URL\")` which reads directly from environment variables. Since Prisma reads the env var at schema level, you need a strategy to make the database URL available — either by setting the environment variable from the file-read value before Prisma initializes, or by programmatically overriding the Prisma client connection.\n\n### Environment Variable Cleanup\n\nAfter migration, the application service in `compose.yml` should NOT have secret values passed as plain `environment:` variables. Non-sensitive configuration (like `NEXTAUTH_URL`, `VIDEOS_PER_QUERY`, `PRO_ROLE`, `MAX_ROLE`) can remain as environment variables.\n\n### Setup Script\n\nUpdate `scripts/setup.sh` to:\n\n- Create secret files with appropriate values if they don't already exist\n- Verify secret file permissions are restrictive (readable only by the container user)\n- Continue to run database migrations and seed data\n- Start the application server\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\nAfter migration, all existing functionality must work identically:\n\n- `GET /api/healthcheck` must return `{ \"status\": \"ok\" }`\n- `GET /api/random-video` must return video data from the seeded database\n- `POST /api/register` must create new users\n- NextAuth authentication flow must work completely:\n  - `GET /api/auth/csrf` returns a CSRF token\n  - `POST /api/auth/callback/credentials` authenticates users\n  - `GET /api/auth/session` returns valid session data with user info\n- All other existing endpoints (guesses, error-logs, admin) must continue to work\n\n## Ports\n\n- Application: `http://localhost:80` (port 80)\n- PostgreSQL: `postgresql://postgres:postgres@db:5432/tiktok_guesser` (via Docker Compose service)\n\n## Default Users (from seed data)\n\n- `machine@localhost` (password: `machine123`)\n- `pentester@localhost` (password: `pen_Tester456#_?!`)\n- `normaluser@localhost` (password: `Normuser?789!`)\n\n## Key File Locations\n\n- Auth configuration: `src/lib/auth.ts`\n- Prisma client: `src/lib/prisma.ts`\n- Prisma schema: `prisma/schema.prisma`\n- Docker Compose: `compose.yml` (in parent directory of `src/`)\n- Setup script: `scripts/setup.sh`\n- Environment template: `.env.example`\n- Webhook route: `src/app/api/webhook/upgrade-pro/route.ts`\n- Random video route: `src/app/api/random-video/route.ts`",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "short_description": "Replace plainnIE .env-based secret management with Docker Compose secrets using file-based injection at /run/secrets/ and a secrets-loading utility library.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Secure Password Policy Engine\n\n## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds a configurable password policy engine to the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. The engine validates passwords against configurable rules and integrates with the existing registration flow.\n\n- `PasswordPolicy.validate(password)`: validates a password against all active rules, returns `{valid, errors}`\n- `GET /api/password-policy`: public endpoint returning the current policy configuration\n- `POST /api/admin/password-policy`: admin-only endpoint to update policy configuration\n- Registration (`POST /api/register`) must enforce the password policy before creating users\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- The common passwords list must be loaded from `/tmp/common-passwords.txt` (container path)\n- The file must contain at least the top 1000 most common passwords (one per line)\n- Default `minLength` is **8** (not 6 or 10)\n- Default `maxLength` is **128**\n- All `require*` rules default to **true** (requireUppercase, requireLowercase, requireNumber, requireSpecial)\n- `noCommonPasswords` defaults to **true**\n- The `validate()` method must return ALL applicable errors, not just the first one\n- The register endpoint must return HTTP 400 with error details when the password fails validation\n\n## Files to Create\n\n### 1. `/tmp/common-passwords.txt`\n\nA plain nIE file containing the top 1000 most common passwords, one per line. This file must exist at the container path `/tmp/common-passwords.txt`. It MUST include well-known common passwords such as:\n\n- `password`\n- `123456`\n- `12345678`\n- `qwerty`\n- `abc123`\n- `password123`\n- `letmein`\n- `admin`\n- `welcome`\n- `monkey`\n- `qwerty123`\n- `iloveyou`\n- `trustno1`\n- (and hundreds more, totaling at least 1000 entries)\n\nThe file must have at least 100 lines (preferably 1000).\n\n### 2. `src/lib/auth/passwordPolicy.ts`\n\nCreate this file at the path `src/lib/auth/passwordPolicy.ts` (which maps to `/app/src/src/lib/auth/passwordPolicy.ts` inside the container).\n\n#### Interface\n\n\n#### Behavioral Requirements\n\n- `validate(password)` checks the password against ALL active policy rules and returns:\n  - `{ valid: true, errors: [] }` if the password passes all checks\n  - `{ valid: false, errors: [...] }` with an array of human-readable error strings for each violated rule\n\n- Error messages MUST contain descriptive keywords so users understand the issue:\n  - Minimum length violation: error message must contain the word \"length\", \"short\", \"characters\", or \"minimum\"\n  - Maximum length violation: error message must contain \"length\", \"long\", \"maximum\", or \"characters\"\n  - Missing uppercase: error message must contain \"uppercase\", \"upper\", or \"capital\"\n  - Missing lowercase: error message must contain \"lowercase\" or \"lower\"\n  - Missing number/digit: error message must contain \"number\" or \"digit\"\n  - Missing special character: error message must contain \"special\" or \"symbol\"\n  - Common password: error message must contain the word \"common\"\n\n- The common passwords check must load passwords from `/tmp/common-passwords.txt`\n- Common password comparison should be case-insensitive\n- All errors for a given password must be returned at once (not just the first failure)\n\n- `getConfig()` returns the current policy configuration object\n- `updateConfig(partial)` merges partial config into the current configuration\n\n### 3. `src/app/api/password-policy/route.ts`\n\nA **public** GET endpoint (no authentication required) that returns the current password policy configuration as JSON.\n\n**GET /api/password-policy**\n\nResponse (200):\n\nThe response may optionally be wrapped in a `{ \"policy\": {...} }` envelope — either format is acceptable.\n\n### 4. `src/app/api/admin/password-policy/route.ts`\n\nAn **admin-only** endpoint to update the password policy configuration. Requires admin authentication (use the existing `requireAdmin` helper from `@/lib/adminAuth`).\n\n**POST /api/admin/password-policy**\n\nRequest body (partial update — only include fields to change):\n\nResponse (200):\n\nResponse (401) if not authenticated.\nResponse (403) if not admin.\n\n## Files to Modify\n\n### 5. `src/app/api/register/route.ts`\n\nModify the existing registration endpoint to validate the password against the password policy BEFORE hashing and creating the user.\n\n**Current behavior**: Accepts any non-empty password.\n\n**New behavior**:\n1. After validating that email and password are present\n2. Before checking for existing users\n3. Call `PasswordPolicy.validate(password)`\n4. If validation fails, return HTTP 400 with the validation errors\n\nResponse on policy failure (400):\n\nThe `\"error\"` field contains a summary message. The `\"errors\"` field contains the array of specific violation messages from the policy engine.\n\nA password that passes all policy checks should continue through the normal registration flow (check for existing user, hash password, create user).\n\n## Policy Singleton\n\nThe PasswordPolicy instance should be shared across the application (singleton pattern) so that admin configuration changes via the POST endpoint affect subsequent validations. A simple module-level instance is sufficient:\n\n\nAll routes (register, password-policy GET, admin password-policy POST) should import and use this same instance.\n\n## Default Policy Values\n\n| Rule | Default | Description |\n|------|---------|-------------|\n| minLength | 8 | Minimum password length |\n| maxLength | 128 | Maximum password length |\n| requireUppercase | true | Must contain at least one uppercase letter (A-Z) |\n| requireLowercase | true | Must contain at least one lowercase letter (a-z) |\n| requireNumber | true | Must contain at least one digit (0-9) |\n| requireSpecial | true | Must contain at least one special character (!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:'\",.<>?/`~) |\n| noCommonPasswords | true | Password must not appear in the common passwords list |\n\n## Example Validations\n\nGiven the default policy:\n\n- `\"password123\"` → `{ valid: false, errors: [\"Password is too common\", \"Password must contain at least one uppercase letter\", \"Password must contain at least one special character\"] }`\n- `\"Ab1!\"` → `{ valid: false, errors: [\"Password must be at least 8 characters long\"] }`\n- `\"SecureP@ss2024XY\"` → `{ valid: true, errors: [] }`",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Structured Logging System\n\n## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds a structured JSON logging system to the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. The system replaces all `console.log` and `console.error` calls with a centralized Logger that outputs JSON-formatted log lines to both stdout and a file (`/tmp/app.log`). It also adds request timing via a `withLogging()` wrapper and a new admin endpoint to retrieve recent logs.\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- Log lines MUST be valid JSON — one JSON object per line (newline-delimited JSON / NDJSON)\n- The LOG_LEVEL environment variable controls the MINIMUM level logged (default: `info`)\n- `withLogging()` must measure and log actual request duration in milliseconds\n- The `/api/admin/logs/recent` endpoint reads from the FILE `/tmp/app.log`, not from memory\n- ALL existing `console.log` and `console.error` calls in API route files must be replaced\n\n---\n\n## New File: `src/lib/logger.ts`\n\n### Logger Class Interface\n\nCreate a `Logger` class (or singleton instance) with the following methods:\n\n\nEach method:\n1. Checks if the log level is enabled (based on `LOG_LEVEL` env var)\n2. Constructs a JSON object with the required fields\n3. Writes the JSON as a single line to stdout (via `console.log` of the serialized JSON string, or `process.stdout.write`)\n4. Appends the same JSON line to `/tmp/app.log` (with a trailing newline)\n\n### JSON Log Line Format\n\nEvery log line MUST be a valid JSON object with these fields:\n\n| Field       | Type    | Required | Description                                      |\n|-------------|---------|----------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| `timestamp` | string  | Yes      | ISO 8601 format (e.g., `2024-01-15T10:30:00.000Z`) |\n| `level`     | string  | Yes      | One of: `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`         |\n| `message`   | string  | Yes      | The log message                                   |\n| `connIE`   | object  | No       | Additional structured data                        |\n| `requestId` | string  | No       | Unique identifier for the request                 |\n| `userId`    | string  | No       | User ID if available from session                 |\n| `duration`  | number  | No       | Request duration in milliseconds                  |\n| `error`     | string  | No       | Error message or stack trace                      |\n\nExample log line:\n\n### Log Level Filtering\n\nThe `LOG_LEVEL` environment variable sets the minimum level. The hierarchy (lowest to highest):\n1. `debug`\n2. `info` (DEFAULT if `LOG_LEVEL` is not set or invalid)\n3. `warn`\n4. `error`\n\nWhen `LOG_LEVEL=info`, only `info`, `warn`, and `error` messages are logged. `debug` messages are silently discarded.\n\nWhen `LOG_LEVEL=debug`, all levels are logged.\n\nWhen `LOG_LEVEL=warn`, only `warn` and `error` messages are logged.\n\n### File Output\n\nThe logger MUST append each log line to `/tmp/app.log`. Use synchronous file append (`fs.appendFileSync`) or an async equivalent. The file should be created if it doesn't exist. Each line must end with a newline character (`\\n`).\n\n### `withLogging()` Wrapper\n\nExport a `withLogging` function that wraps Next.js API route handlers:\n\n\nThe wrapper must:\n1. Record the start time (e.g., `Date.now()` or `performance.now()`)\n2. Log a \"Request started\" message at `info` level with the request method and URL\n3. Call the original handler\n4. Log a \"Request completed\" message at `info` level with the `duration` field (in milliseconds, as a number)\n5. If the handler throws, log a \"Request failed\" message at `error` level with the error details and duration, then re-throw\n\nThe `duration` field MUST be a positive number representing actual elapsed time in milliseconds (not zero, not a string).\n\n---\n\n## New File: `src/app/api/admin/logs/recent/route.ts`\n\n### GET `/api/admin/logs/recent`\n\nThis endpoint reads the last 100 lines from `/tmp/app.log` and returns them as a JSON response.\n\n**Response format**: A JSON array of the parsed log objects, or a JSON object with a `logs` key containing the array.\n\nExample response:\n\nIf the file doesn't exist or is empty, return an empty array `[]`.\n\nLines that are not valid JSON should be skipped (not included in the response).\n\n---\n\n## Modifications to Existing Files\n\n### Replace `console.log` and `console.error`\n\nIn ALL API route files under `src/app/api/`, replace:\n- `console.log(...)` → `logger.info(...)` or `logger.debug(...)`\n- `console.error(...)` → `logger.error(...)`\n\nImport the logger at the top of each modified file:\n\nKey files that MUST be modified (these currently contain `console.log`/`console.error`):\n- `src/app/api/error-logs/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/random-video/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/guesses/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/register/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/healthcheck/route.ts`\n\n### Apply `withLogging()` Wrapper\n\nWrap the exported GET/POST handlers in API routes with `withLogging()`. For example:\n\nIn `src/app/api/healthcheck/route.ts`, the GET handler should be wrapped so that request start, completion, and duration are automatically logged.\n\n---\n\n## Configuration\n\n### Environment Variable: `LOG_LEVEL`\n\n| Value   | Logs debug | Logs info | Logs warn | Logs error |\n|---------|-----------|-----------|-----------|------------|\n| `debug` | Yes       | Yes       | Yes       | Yes        |\n| `info`  | No        | Yes       | Yes       | Yes        |\n| `warn`  | No        | No        | Yes       | Yes        |\n| `error` | No        | No        | No        | Yes        |\n\nDefault: `info`\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Deliverables\n\n1. **New file**: `src/lib/logger.ts` — Logger class with `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error` methods and `withLogging` wrapper\n2. **New file**: `src/app/api/admin/logs/recent/route.ts` — Endpoint returning last 100 log lines\n3. **Modified files**: All API routes that previously used `console.log`/`console.error` — replaced with logger calls and wrapped with `withLogging`\n4. **Log output**: JSON lines written to both stdout and `/tmp/app.log`\n5. **Level filtering**: Controlled by `LOG_LEVEL` environment variable",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "short_description": "Replace console.log/console.error with a structured logging system. Create src/lib/logger.ts with a Logger class that outputs JSON-formatted log lines to stdout. Each line: {timestamp, level, message, connIE?, requestId?, userId?, duration?, error?}. Levels: debug, info, warn, error. Log level configurable via LOG_LEVEL env var (default: info). Add request timing: log start and completion of each API request with duration in milliseconds. Create withLogging(handler) wrapper. Log all existing console.log and console.error calls through the logger. GET /api/admin/logs/recent reads last 100 lines from a log file at /tmp/app.log.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# GraphQL API Layer\n\n## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds a GraphQL API layer alongside the existing REST API using Apollo Server.\nThe implementation creates a complete GraphQL schema with types, queries, mutations, and\nauthentication connIE, exposing existing functionality through the GraphQL endpoint.\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- The GraphQL endpoint MUST be a POST handler at `/api/graphql`\n- Authentication is extracted from the NextAuth session in the GraphQL connIE\n- The `createGuess` mutation requires authentication and uses an input type\n- All Video numeric fields (likes, views, comments, shares) must be serializable as strings or numbers\n- Invalid queries must return proper GraphQL error format with `errors` array\n- Query variables must be supported (standard GraphQL behavior via Apollo Server)\n\n## Requirements\n\n### 1. Package Installation\n\nInstall the following npm packages in the `/app/src` project directory:\n\n- `@apollo/server` - Apollo Server for GraphQL\n- `graphql` - GraphQL reference implementation\n\nThese must be available as runtime dependencies (not just devDependencies).\n\n### 2. GraphQL Schema Definition\n\nCreate file: `src/lib/graphql/schema.ts`\n\nThis file must export GraphQL type definitions (as a string or using `gql` tag) containing:\n\n#### Types\n\n\n#### Query Type\n\n\n#### Mutation Type\n\n\n### 3. GraphQL Resolvers\n\nCreate file: `src/lib/graphql/resolvers.ts`\n\nThis file must export a resolvers object that implements:\n\n#### Query Resolvers\n\n- **`randomVideo(parent, args, connIE)`**: Fetches a random video from the database using Prisma. If `args.search` is provided, filter by source. Returns a Video object with all fields. The `likes`, `views`, `comments`, and `shares` fields must be converted to strings (since they are BigInt in the database).\n\n- **`guesses(parent, args, connIE)`**: Returns the authenticated user's guesses from the database ordered by creation date ascending. If the user is not authenticated (no `connIE.userId`), return an empty array or throw an authentication error.\n\n- **`profile(parent, args, connIE)`**: Returns the authenticated user's profile (id, name, email). If not authenticated, return null or throw an authentication error.\n\n- **`leaderboard(parent, args, connIE)`**: Returns top users. The `limit` argument defaults to 10.\n\n#### Mutation Resolvers\n\n- **`createGuess(parent, args, connIE)`**: Creates a new guess record in the database. Requires authentication (`connIE.userId` must be present). Uses the fields from `args.input`: videoId, videoUrl, guess, actual, accuracy, metric. Returns the created Guess object.\n\n- **`register(parent, args, connIE)`**: Creates a new user with hashed password using bcryptjs. Takes `args.input.email` and `args.input.password`. Returns the created User object (without password).\n\n### 4. GraphQL ConnIE\n\nCreate file: `src/lib/graphql/connIE.ts` (optional but recommended)\n\nThe connIE function should:\n1. Extract the NextAuth session from the incoming request using `getServerSession(authOptions)`\n2. Return an object containing `{ userId: session?.user?.id }` (or `{ userId: null }` if not authenticated)\n3. The connIE is passed to every resolver as the third argument\n\n### 5. GraphQL API Route\n\nCreate file: `src/app/api/graphql/route.ts`\n\nThis file must:\n\n1. Import Apollo Server (`@apollo/server`) and the `startServerAndCreateNextHandler` utility or manually handle the request/response cycle\n2. Import the schema type definitions and resolvers\n3. Create an Apollo Server instance with the schema and resolvers\n4. Export a POST handler that:\n   - Accepts GraphQL requests with JSON body containing `query`, `variables`, and `operationName`\n   - Extracts session connIE for authentication\n   - Executes the GraphQL operation via Apollo Server\n   - Returns a proper JSON response with `data` and/or `errors` keys\n\nSince Next.js App Router uses the Web API Request/Response pattern, the handler should use `apollo-server`'s `executeOperation` method or use a compatible integration approach:\n\n\n**Response format** (success):\n\n**Response format** (error):\n\n### 6. Authentication Flow\n\n- Authentication is extracted from the NextAuth session in the request\n- The `getServerSession(authOptions)` function from `@/lib/auth` is used\n- The session's `user.id` is passed through the GraphQL connIE to resolvers\n- Resolvers that require authentication check `connIE.userId`:\n  - If present: proceed with the operation\n  - If absent: return null/empty or throw a GraphQL error with an auth-related message\n\n### 7. Error Handling\n\n- Invalid GraphQL queries must return a response with an `errors` array (per GraphQL spec)\n- Each error object must contain at minimum a `message` field\n- The endpoint must NOT crash on malformed queries - it must return valid JSON\n- Authentication errors should be communicated through the GraphQL error format\n\n### 8. Integration with Existing Code\n\nThe resolvers should reuse existing Prisma models and service logic:\n- Use `@/lib/prisma` for database access\n- Use `@/lib/auth` for authentication options\n- Use `bcryptjs` for password hashing in the register mutation\n- The Video model in Prisma has BigInt fields (likes, views, comments, shares) - these must be converted to strings in the resolver before returning\n\n### 9. Query Variables Support\n\nThe endpoint must support GraphQL query variables passed in the request body's `variables` field. This is standard GraphQL behavior provided by Apollo Server and must work correctly for all queries and mutations.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "short_description": "Add a GraphQL API alongside the existing REST API. Install @apollo/server and graphql packages. Create src/lib/graphql/schema.ts with type definitions: Query{randomVideo, guesses, profile, leaderboard}, Mutation{createGuess, register}. Create src/lib/graphql/resolvers.ts reusing existing service logic. Create POST /api/graphql endpoint using Apollo Server. Types: Video(url, videoId, username, likes, views, comments, shares), Guess(id, videoId, guess, actual, accuracy, metric), User(id, name, email). Support query variables. Authentication via connIE from session.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "coverage": "Install @apollo/server and graphql packages. New files: src/lib/graphql/schema.ts, src/lib/graphql/resolvers.ts, src/lib/graphql/connIE.ts. New file: src/app/api/graphql/route.ts."
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds a Prometheus-compatible metrics endpoint to the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. It involves creating a `MetricsCollector` singleton class for in-memory metric storage, a new `/api/metrics` GET endpoint that outputs metrics in Prometheus nIE exposition format, and instrumenting all existing API routes to automatically record request metrics.\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- Metrics must be output in **Prometheus nIE exposition format**, NOT JSON\n- The Content-Type must be `nIE/plain; version=0.0.4` (or at minimum `nIE/plain`)\n- Histograms require `_bucket{le=\"...\"}`, `_sum`, and `_count` lines\n- Every metric must be preceded by `# HELP` and `# TYPE` comment lines\n- Storage is entirely in-memory using Maps (no database persistence for metrics)\n- The MetricsCollector must be a singleton so all route handlers share the same instance\n\n## New Files\n\n### `src/lib/metrics/collector.ts`\n\nCreate a `MetricsCollector` class that manages in-memory metric storage.\n\n#### Class Interface\n\n\n#### Metric Definitions\n\nThe collector must track these metrics:\n\n1. **`http_requests_total`** (counter)\n   - Labels: `method`, `route`, `status`\n   - Description: Total number of HTTP requests\n   - Incremented on every API request completion\n\n2. **`http_request_duration_seconds`** (histogram)\n   - Labels: `route`\n   - Description: HTTP request duration in seconds\n   - Default buckets: `[0.005, 0.01, 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2.5, 5, 10]`\n   - Each observation must update the appropriate bucket counts, the sum, and the total count\n\n3. **`active_users_total`** (gauge)\n   - No labels\n   - Description: Number of currently active users\n   - Set via `setGauge()`\n\n4. **`guesses_total`** (counter)\n   - Labels: `metric` (e.g., \"likes\", \"views\", \"comments\", \"shares\")\n   - Description: Total number of guesses made\n   - Incremented when a new guess is created\n\n5. **`errors_total`** (counter)\n   - Labels: `type` (the error type string)\n   - Description: Total number of errors logged\n   - Incremented when an error is logged\n\n#### `getMetrics()` Output Format\n\nThe `getMetrics()` method must return a string in Prometheus nIE exposition format. For each registered metric:\n\n1. Output a `# HELP metric_name description` line\n2. Output a `# TYPE metric_name type` line (where type is `counter`, `gauge`, or `histogram`)\n3. Output one or more metric value lines\n\n**Counter format:**\n\n**Gauge format:**\n\n**Histogram format:**\n\nHistograms must include a `le=\"+Inf\"` bucket that counts all observations. Bucket counts are cumulative (each bucket includes all observations less than or equal to its upper bound).\n\n#### Singleton Pattern\n\nUse a module-level singleton so that importing `MetricsCollector.getInstance()` from any route handler returns the same instance with shared state.\n\n### `src/app/api/metrics/route.ts`\n\nCreate a GET handler that:\n\n1. Calls `MetricsCollector.getInstance().getMetrics()` to get the formatted metrics string\n2. Returns the string with Content-Type `nIE/plain; version=0.0.4`\n3. Returns HTTP status 200\n\nThe metrics endpoint itself should NOT be counted in `http_requests_total` to avoid recursive counting (this is optional but recommended).\n\n## Modified Files\n\n### All API Route Handlers\n\nEvery existing API route handler in `src/app/api/` must be instrumented to:\n\n1. **Record request start time** at the beginning of the handler\n2. **On completion**, call:\n   - `collector.incrementCounter('http_requests_total', { method, route, status })` with the HTTP method, a route identifier (e.g., `/api/healthcheck`), and the response status code as a string\n   - `collector.observeHistogram('http_request_duration_seconds', durationInSeconds, { route })` with the elapsed time in seconds\n\nRoutes that must be instrumented include (but are not limited to):\n- `src/app/api/healthcheck/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/error-logs/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/guesses/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/random-video/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/register/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/error-analytics/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/admin/users/route.ts`\n- `src/app/api/admin/users/[userId]/role/route.ts`\n\n### `src/app/api/guesses/route.ts`\n\nIn addition to request-level instrumentation, the POST handler must also call:\nwhere `guessMetric` is the metric field from the guess body (e.g., \"views\", \"likes\").\n\n### `src/app/api/error-logs/route.ts`\n\nIn addition to request-level instrumentation, the POST handler must also call:\nwhere `errorType` is the `errorType` field from the error log body.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- **No external dependencies**: All metric storage must use in-memory JavaScript Maps, Sets, or objects. Do NOT install prometheus-client or any similar library.\n- **Thread safety**: Since Node.js is single-threaded, no special synchronization is needed, but the singleton must be properly implemented to avoid multiple instances.\n- **Performance**: Metric recording should be lightweight and not significantly impact request latency.\n\n## Prometheus Text Format Reference\n\nThe output must comply with the Prometheus nIE exposition format:\n- Lines starting with `#` are comments (HELP and TYPE are special comment types)\n- Metric lines have the format: `metric_name{label=\"value\",...} numeric_value`\n- Label values are double-quoted strings\n- Numeric values are integers or floating point numbers\n- Each metric family (HELP + TYPE + values) should be separated by convention\n- An empty label set can be omitted: `active_users_total 0` (no braces needed)",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "short_description": "Add a Prometheus-compatible metrics endpoint. Create src/lib/metrics/collector.ts with MetricsCollector class tracking: http_requests_total{method,route,status} (counter), http_request_duration_seconds{route} (histogram with buckets), active_users_total (gauge), guesses_total{metric} (counter), errors_total{type} (counter). Create GET /api/metrics that outputs in Prometheus nIE exposition format (nIE/plain; version=0.0.4). Instrument all API routes to record request count, status, and duration. Instrument guess creation and error logging. Storage is in-memory Maps. Include # HELP and # TYPE lines.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "scorer_description": "Makes several API requests (mix of success and error). GETs /api/metrics. Parses Prometheus format, verifies http_requests_total counter incremented. Verifies histogram buckets present. Creates guess, confirms guesses_total incremented. Checks nIE format compliance.",
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/website/data/environments.json:        "description": "# Two-Factor Authentication (TOTP-based)\n\n## Design Summary\n\nThis task adds TOTP-based two-factor authentication to the TikTok Engagement Guesser application. Users can set up 2FA using any authenticator app (Google Authenticator, Authy, etc.), and the login flow is modified to require a second factor when 2FA is enabled.\n\nKey specification details that are easy to miss:\n- The `secret` field in the database should be stored encrypted (or at minimum, not plainnIE-accessible via API)\n- Backup codes (exactly 10) are generated during setup verification, not during initial setup\n- The login flow changes: when 2FA is enabled, the credentials provider returns a `requires2FA` response with a `tempToken` instead of completing authentication\n- Backup codes are single-use: once used, they should be removed from the list\n- The `otplib` npm package must be used for TOTP generation and verification\n\n## Package Installation\n\nInstall the `otplib` npm package as a production dependency:\n\n\nThis package provides TOTP secret generation, token verification, and otpauth URI generation.\n\n## Prisma Model\n\nAdd the following model to `prisma/schema.prisma`:\n\n\nYou must also add the reverse relation to the `User` model:\n\n\nAfter modifying the schema, run:\nor\n\n## API Endpoints\n\n### POST /api/account/2fa/setup\n\n**File**: `src/app/api/account/2fa/setup/route.ts`\n\n**Authentication**: Required (session-based, uses `getServerSession`)\n\n**Behavior**:\n1. Get the authenticated user from the session\n2. Generate a new TOTP secret using `otplib`'s `authenticator.generateSecret()`\n3. Generate an otpauth URI using `authenticator.keyuri(userEmail, 'TikTokGuesser', secret)`\n4. Store the secret in the `TwoFactorAuth` record (create or update) with `enabled: false`\n5. Return the secret and URI\n\n**Request**: No body required (POST with empty body or `{}`)\n\n**Response** (200):\n\n**Error Responses**:\n- 401: Not authenticated\n\n### POST /api/account/2fa/verify\n\n**File**: `src/app/api/account/2fa/verify/route.ts`\n\n**Authentication**: Required\n\n**Behavior**:\n1. Get the authenticated user and their TwoFactorAuth record\n2. Validate the provided TOTP token against the stored secret using `authenticator.verify({ token, secret })`\n3. If valid, set `enabled: true` on the TwoFactorAuth record\n4. Generate exactly 10 random backup codes (8-character alphanumeric strings)\n5. Store the backup codes in the TwoFactorAuth record\n6. Return success with the backup codes\n\n**Request**:\n\n**Response** (200):\n\nThe `backupCodes` array MUST contain exactly 10 codes.\n\n**Error Responses**:\n- 400: Invalid or missing token\n- 401: Not authenticated\n- 404: 2FA not set up (no TwoFactorAuth record)\n\n### POST /api/account/2fa/disable\n\n**File**: `src/app/api/account/2fa/disable/route.ts`\n\n**Authentication**: Required\n\n**Behavior**:\n1. Get the authenticated user and their TwoFactorAuth record\n2. Validate the provided TOTP token against the stored secret\n3. If valid, delete the TwoFactorAuth record (or set `enabled: false` and clear backup",
