/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-v2.md:**Purpose**: Test whether a monitor that sees cross-task connIE catches more.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-v2.md:3. **Equal total agent compute**: The diffuse attack gets N agent runs but each has a fresh connIE and works on a real MT. The concentrated attack gets 1 agent run. To control for total compute, we can also compare against a concentrated attack with N*budget tokens. (Though in practice, concentrated attacks are already token-budget-limited by the single task's complexity.)
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-v2.md:### Monitor ConnIE as Blue Team Affordance (The Core of This Research)
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-design.md:### Important connIE
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-design.md:Our design sits at an interesting point: by giving the attacker multiple MTs in one episode with persistent state, the monitor accumulates connIE that could enable **pattern-based incrimination**. The empirical question is whether this closes the gap that makes diffuse threats hard.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/spar/control-tower-multi-agent-squad/research_wiki/multi-task-episodes-design.md:3. **Is this just N independent single-task evals?** No — persistent state, accumulated connIE, and cross-task planning fundamentally change both attack and defense strategies.
