/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:Diffusion models generate images and videos by learning to reverse a noise process, starting from random noise and iteratively refining it into coherent visual output guided by a nIE prompt. Pretrained diffusion models, such as [FLUX.1-dev](https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-dev) for images or [Wan 2.1](https://huggingface.co/Wan-AI/Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers) for video, produce impressive general-purpose results, but they know nothing about your particular visual domain, style, or subject matter. Fine-tuning bridges that gap: you adapt the model on your own data so it produces outputs that match your requirements, without the cost of training from scratch.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:Under the hood, NeMo AutoModel uses [flow matching](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02747), a modern generative framework that learns to transform noise into data by regressing a velocity field along straight interpolation paths. It integrates with [Hugging Face Diffusers](https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers) to provide distributed fine-tuning for nIE-to-image and nIE-to-video models. This guide walks you through the process end to end, from installation through training and inference, using [Wan 2.1 T2V 1.3B](https://huggingface.co/Wan-AI/Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers) as a running example.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:```nIE
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:- Text embeddings produced by a nIE encoder from the associated captions or prompts
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:2. A YAML configuration file — a nIE file in YAML format that specifies all settings the recipe uses: which model to fine-tune, where the data lives, optimizer hyperparameters, parallelism strategy, etc.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:2. A YAML configuration file is a nIE file in YAML format that specifies all settings the recipe uses: which model to fine-tune, where the data lives, optimizer hyperparameters, parallelism strategy, and other settings. You customize training by editing this file rather than modifying code, allowing you to scale seamlessly from 1 to hundreds of GPUs.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:  cp_size: 1      # ConnIE parallelism
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:| **High-quality image generation** | [FLUX.1-dev](#flux1-dev-nIE-to-image) | State-of-the-art nIE-to-image with 12B params and guidance-based control |
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:- **Dataloader**: `build_nIE_to_image_multiresolution_dataloader`
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:After training is complete, you can use the model to generate images or videos from nIE prompts. This step is called inference. Unlike training, where the model learns from data, inference is where it produces new outputs.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/diffusion/finetune.mdx:In diffusion models, generation works by starting from random noise and iteratively denoising it, guided by your nIE prompt, until a clean image or video emerges.
