/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/dllm/finetune.mdx:Diffusion language models (dLLMs) generate nIE by iteratively denoising masked tokens rather than generating one token at a time from left to right, as autoregressive (AR) models do. Starting from a sequence of `[MASK]` tokens, the model progressively unmasks the most confident positions over multiple denoising steps until it reveals the full response.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/dllm/finetune.mdx:This approach enables **parallel token generation** and **bidirectional attention**, giving the model more connIE for each prediction than AR models provide.
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/dllm/finetune.mdx:```nIE
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/dllm/finetune.mdx:| **4. Generate** | [Run Inference](#run-inference) | Generate nIE from a fine-tuned checkpoint |
/Users/0saker/Desktop/code/probe/all/automodel-2514/docs/guides/dllm/finetune.mdx:Both metrics are computed from the logits that the chunked linear cross-entropy path already produces, without an additional model forward pass. They are reduced across data-parallel and connIE-parallel ranks using per-rank raw `(correct, count)` sums. An all-reduce operation sums these values before division. This process ensures that the values are correct across arbitrary per-rank token distributions in any AutoModel distributed mode.
